## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests the ability to model selections of multiple disjoint subarrays with an exact count — a common dynamic programming pattern in interviews.

## Key Concepts
- **DP on prefix**: State dp[i][t] tracks the best total using the first i elements with exactly t subarrays
- **Prefix sums**: Enable O(1) calculation of any subarray sum
- **Backpointers**: Store decisions to reconstruct the chosen subarrays
- **Edge cases**: Handle when n < k or when all values are negative

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) pref[i] = pref[i - 1] + A[i - 1];

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prev = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(null));
  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      if (dp[i - 1][t] >= dp[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = dp[i - 1][t];
        prev[i][t] = { skip: true };
      }
      for (let l = 1; l <= i; l++) {
        if (t > 0 && dp[l - 1][t - 1] !== -Infinity) {
          const val = dp[l - 1][t - 1] + (pref[i] - pref[l - 1]);
          if (val > dp[i][t]) {
            dp[i][t] = val;
            prev[i][t] = { skip: false, l, t: t - 1 };
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const result = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (i > 0 && t > 0) {
    const p = prev[i][t];
    if (p.skip) { i--; continue; }
    result.push([p.l, i]);
    i = p.l - 1; t = p.t;
  }
  return { value: dp[n][k], subarrays: result.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to craft DP with interval constraints and reconstruction using palindromic substrings.

## Key Concepts
- Palindromic substring precomputation in O(n^2)
- DP over prefix length and exact count: dp[i][t]
- Transition considers ending a palindrome at i with start p
- Reconstruction requires backpointers to recover substrings

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPalSubstr(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const isPal = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(false));
  for (let i = n - 1; i >= 0; --i) {
    for (let j = i; j < n; ++j) {
      if (s[i] === s[j] && (j - i < 2 || isPal[i+1][j-1])) isPal[i][j] = true;
    }
  }
  const NEG = -1e9;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i][t], dp[i-1][t]);
      if (t > 0) {
        for (let p = 0; p < i; p++) {
          if (isPal[p][i-1]) {
            const val = dp[p][t-1] + (i - p);
            if (val > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = p; }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // Reconstruction would trace par[] to extract substrings
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to O(nk) space?
- How would you reconstruct the exact substrings efficiently without extra passes?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests mastery of DAG dynamic programming, vertex-disjoint path packing, and careful state design to enforce disjointness while meeting exact cardinality constraints.

## Key Concepts
- DAG topological ordering enables DP by ensuring all dependencies are processed first
- Vertex-disjoint paths are guaranteed by tracking endpoint-specific states
- Path reconstruction requires storing predecessor pointers during computation
- Complexity: O(Mk + Nk) time, O(Nk) space

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode
const topo = topologicalSort(dag);
const dp = Array(N).fill(null).map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
const pred = Array(N).fill(null).map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(null));

for (const v of topo) {
  dp[v][0] = 0;
  const bestPrev = Math.max(...dp[v]);

  for (const u of dag.inEdges(v)) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      if (dp[u][t] > -Infinity) {
        // Extend existing path
        if (dp[u][t] + val[v] > dp[v][t]) {
          dp[v][t] = dp[u][t] + val[v];
          pred[v][t] = u;
        }
        // Start new path
        if (t < k && bestPrev + val[v] > dp[v][t + 1]) {
          dp[v][t + 1] = bestPrev + val[v];
          pred[v][t + 1] = u;
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

const answer = Math.max(...dp.map(v => v[k]));
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to model multi-block dynamic programming with non-trivial block scoring that depends on range statistics (max, min) and block length. It also covers reconstruction of the exact blocks, and invites discussion of time/space trade-offs.

## Key Concepts

- Partition DP with fixed k blocks
- Block score uses range (max-min) and length
- Inline maintenance of max/min while expanding the last block
- Path reconstruction with predecessor pointers

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxScore(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const parent = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      let curMax = -INF, curMin = INF;
      for (let l = i; l >= 1; l--) {
        curMax = Math.max(curMax, A[l-1]);
        curMin = Math.min(curMin, A[l-1]);
        const blockScore = (curMax - curMin) * (i - l + 1);
        if (dp[l-1][t-1] + blockScore > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = dp[l-1][t-1] + blockScore;
          parent[i][t] = l-1;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct blocks
  const blocks = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0) {
    const l = parent[i][t] + 1;
    blocks.unshift([l, i]);
    i = l - 1;
    t--;
  }

  return {maxScore: dp[n][k], blocks};
}
```

## Time/Space Complexity

Time: O(n²k) - for each i and t, we scan O(n) possible l values while maintaining max/min.

Space: O(nk) for dp table plus O(nk) for parent pointers if reconstruction needed, otherwise O(nk).

## Why This Is Asked
Tests real DP design with an extra state (discount used) and careful recurrence.

## Key Concepts
- 2D DP with a binary state (discount used or not)
- Transitions combine taking cost with/without discount
- Space optimization using rolling arrays

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostWithDiscount(grid){
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const INF = 1e15;
  let dp0 = new Array(m).fill(INF);
  let dp1 = new Array(m).fill(INF);
  dp0[0] = grid[0][0];
  dp1[0] = Math.floor(grid[0][0] / 2);
  for (let j = 1; j < m; j++) {
    dp0[j] = grid[0][j] + dp0[j-1];
    dp1[j] = Math.min(grid[0][j] + dp1[j-1], Math.floor(grid[0][j]/2) + dp0[j-1]);
  }
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) {
    dp0[0] += grid[i][0];
    dp1[0] = Math.min(grid[i][0] + dp1[0], Math.floor(grid[i][0]/2) + dp0[0-1]);
    for (let j = 1; j < m; j++) {
      const v = grid[i][j];
      dp1[j] = Math.min(v + Math.min(dp1[j], dp1[j-1]), Math.floor(v/2) + Math.min(dp0[j], dp0[j-1]));
      dp0[j] = v + Math.min(dp0[j], dp0[j-1]);
    }
  }
  return dp1[m-1];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you support multiple discounts or varying discount values?
- How does this adapt if diagonal moves are allowed or costs can be negative?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests budgeted DP on strings and reconstruction. 

## Key Concepts
- dp[i][j][d] encodes a subproblem under a deletion budget; 
- palindrome subsequence logic with matches/skips; 
- reconstruction paths under deletion budget.

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPalWithDeletions(s, k){
  const n=s.length; 
  const dp=Array.from({length:n},()=>Array.from({length:n},()=>Array(k+1).fill(0)));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){ dp[i][i][0]=1; for(let d=1; d<=k; d++) dp[i][i][d]=1; }
  for(let len=2; len<=n; len++){
    for(let i=0; i+len-1<n; i++){
      const j=i+len-1;
      for(let d=0; d<=k; d++){
        if(s[i]===s[j]){
          dp[i][j][d]=Math.max(dp[i+1][j-1][d]+2, (d>0?dp[i+1][j][d-1]:0), (d>0?dp[i][j-1][d-1]:0));
        } else {
          dp[i][j][d]=Math.max(dp[i+1][j][d], dp[i][j-1][d], (d>0?dp[i+1][j-1][d-1]:0));
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return Math.max(...Array.from({length:k+1},(_,d)=>dp[0][n-1][d]));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- Can space be reduced to O(n^2)?
- How to reconstruct the deletion indices?
- Change objective to count distinct palindromic subsequences with budget.

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests your ability to design a dynamic programming solution for fixed-partition subproblems. It requires careful state definition, transition logic, and the ability to handle substring-to-integer conversion efficiently. Additionally, storing cut pointers enables reconstruction of the optimal partition.

## Key Concepts

- **Partition DP**: Divide a linear sequence into exactly k segments
- **On-the-fly value computation**: Accumulate digit values incrementally without re-parsing substrings
- **Reconstruction**: Maintain a parent pointer array to recover the cut positions
- **Time/Space tradeoffs**: O(n²k) time complexity with O(nk) space; can optimize to O(nk) space using rolling arrays

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxSumPartition(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const digits = s.split('').map(Number);

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const cut = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= Math.min(i, k); t++) {
      let val = 0;
      for (let j = i; j >= t; j--) {
        val = digits[j - 1] * Math.pow(10, i - j) + val;
        if (dp[j - 1][t - 1] !== -Infinity) {
          const sum = dp[j - 1][t - 1] + val;
          if (sum > dp[i][t]) {
            dp[i][t] = sum;
            cut[i][t] = j - 1;
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const cuts = [];
  let pos = n;
  for (let t = k; t >= 1; t--) {
    cuts.push(cut[pos][t]);
    pos = cut[pos][t];
  }

  return { maxSum: dp[n][k], cutPositions: cuts.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP setup for segment selection with non-adjacency and reconstruction, a common beginner DP pattern with a twist.

## Key Concepts
- DP over index i and blocks t
- Enforce non-adjacency via s-2 reference
- Prefix sums for O(1) subarray sums
- Reconstruction pointers for endpoints
- Time/space trade-offs; possible optimization to O(nk) time and O(n) space per k

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarraysSum(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const P = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) P[i + 1] = P[i] + A[i];

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const take = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      // skip i
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i][t], dp[i - 1][t]);
      if (t > 0) {
        for (let s = 1; s <= i; s++) {
          const sum = P[i] - P[s - 1];
          const prev = (s >= 2) ? dp[s - 2][t - 1] : (t - 1 === 0 ? 0 : -Infinity);
          const cand = sum + prev;
          if (cand > dp[i][t]) {
            dp[i][t] = cand;
            take[i][t] = s;
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction would trace back using 'take' from (n, bestT)
  return { maxSum: dp[n][0], blocks: [] };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests dynamic programming skills for selecting exactly k disjoint subarrays with a per-subarray penalty—a common variant in coding interviews that extends standard maximum subarray problems.

## Key Concepts

- **Non-overlapping constraint**: Subarrays must not intersect, handled by the state machine.
- **Dual-state DP**: Maintaining both global (dp) and local (end) states to track ongoing vs. completed selections.
- **Reconstruction**: Tracing back decisions to recover actual subarray endpoints.
- **Optimization**: Balancing time complexity (O(nk)) against space (full vs. rolling arrays).

## Code Example

```javascript
const solve = (A, k, c) => {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const end = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const choice = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(null));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      // Not taking a subarray ending at i
      if (dp[i-1][t] >= end[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
        choice[i][t] = 'skip';
      } else {
        dp[i][t] = end[i][t];
        choice[i][t] = 'end';
      }

      // Taking/starting subarray at i
      if (t > 0) {
        const startNew = dp[i-1][t-1] + A[i-1] - c;
        const extend = end[i-1][t] + A[i-1] - c;
        end[i][t] = Math.max(extend, startNew);
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruction logic would go here
  return { value: dp[n][k], subarrays: [] };
};
```

## Complexity

- **Time:** O(nk)
- **Space:** O(nk) for full DP, O(k) for optimized version

## Why This Is Asked
This introduces selecting non-covering, fixed-cost segments, a natural DP with a running max optimization. It tests understanding of prefix sums, state pruning, and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Prefix sums to compute segment sums in O(1)
- DP over i and t with exactly t segments
- Running maximum best[t] to avoid O(n) inner loop
- Path reconstruction to recover endpoints

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxValueSegments(A, k, c){
  const n = A.length;
  const S = Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) S[i]=S[i-1]+A[i-1];
  const negInf = -1e18;
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(negInf));
  const par = Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill([-1,-1]));
  dp[0][0]=0;
  for(let t=1;t<=k;t++) par[0][t]=[-1,-1];
  for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
    let best = negInf; // max of dp[m][t-1]-S[m]
    for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
      best = Math.max(best, dp[i-1][t-1]-S[i-1]);
      const val = best + S[i] - c;
      if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t]=val; par[i][t]=[i-1,-1]; }
      if(dp[i-1][t] > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t]=dp[i-1][t]; par[i][t]=[i-1,t]; }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction (simple version)
  let i=n, t=k, ends=[];
  while(t>0){
    const [pi, pt] = par[i][t] || [-1,-1];
    if(pi===-1) break;
    // approximate end at i, start at some m where dp[m][t-1]-S[m] was max
    // for brevity here, return only values; full reconstruction can be added as needed
    ends.push(i);
    i = pi; t--;
  }
  return { value: dp[n][k], ends: ends.reverse() };
}
```

